<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#libyco-berber-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Libyco-Berber script</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Libyc script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Libyco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> script is an ancient alphabet of consonants used to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> from at least the third century BCE. It is known from thousands of funerary and rock inscriptions, and bilingual Punic texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Dougga</a>, including a dedication to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, made its partial decipherment possible. It is the ancestor of the Tifinagh script of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/libyco-berber-script.jpg" alt="Libyco-Berber script" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prehistory-draa16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
